  "id": "ch0-the-defence",
  "title": "The defence and its ideas",
  "intro": "The Slav answers 1.d4 d5 2.c4 with 2...c6. The pawn on c6 defends d5 so that Black can hold the centre without ever shutting in the bishop on c8. That one detail is the whole opening.\n\n## Why the pawn on c6 and not on e6\n\nThe Queen's Gambit Declined plays 2...e6. It is solid and completely respectable, and it locks the bishop on c8 behind its own pawns, after which Black spends the next fifteen moves trying to free it. The Slav supports d5 from the other side. The bishop leaves the back rank first, and ...e6 follows later once it costs nothing. If you already play the Caro-Kann, this is the same bargain one file across: pawns on c6 and d5, and the light-squared bishop out before the door shuts.\n\n## What White can try\n\nAfter 2...c6 there are five things worth knowing and no more. White develops with 3.Nf3 Nf6 4.Nc3 and Black takes on c4, which is the main line and chapter 1. White builds quietly with 4.e3 or 4.Bf4, chapter 2. White releases the tension with 3.cxd5, chapter 3. White tries a queen move, a fianchetto or a different knight order, chapter 4. Or White never plays c4 at all, which is the London, the Colle and the Torre, and that is chapter 5. Chapter 6 is exercises.\n\n## Your standard plan\n\nIn the main line the plan is four steps. Take the pawn on c4 when it is offered, and let White spend a move winning it back. Put the bishop on f5 while the diagonal is open. Play ...e6, develop the rest and castle. Then break with ...c5 or ...e5 once the pieces are ready.\n\nBlack is not attacking anybody in the first ten moves and does not need to. There are no bad pieces and no weak pawns, and most club opponents run out of plan before Black does.\n\n## What you give up\n\nA move and some space. The pawn on c6 develops nothing, and in the main line Black hands the extra pawn back rather than clinging to it. What Black gets is a position with no problem piece in it, and at club level that is worth more than the tempo.\n\n## Typical mistakes at this level\n\nPlaying ...e6 before the bishop has a square, which turns the Slav into the Queen's Gambit Declined you were avoiding. Trying to hold the c4 pawn with ...b5 when a4 is available, after which axb5 and b3 tear the queenside open. Playing ...Bf5 one move too early, when cxd5 followed by Qb3 hits b7 and d5 at the same time. And forgetting the break: a comfortable Slav with no ...c5 or ...e5 in it goes quietly downhill.",
  "defaultTags": ["development"],
